      <h1 data-split>Margaret</h1>
      <span class="fullname" data-reveal>Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns · 1929 – 2026</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <div class="col prose">
      <p class="first">Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns died peacefully in Montreal on November 5, 2026, surrounded by her family. She was 97.</p>
      <p>To meet Margaret in her prime—which lasted, by most estimates, at least sixty years—was to meet a force. A glamorous redhead with an aristocratic air, she could fill a room before she had properly entered it. Margaret was eloquent and endlessly sociable, quick to laugh and sometimes quicker to anger, with a Scottish wit as sharp as her opinions. She felt things intensely, and those closest to her knew that affection and fury could arrive with equal force, though the latter seldom lasted as long. She loved beautiful things, stimulating conversation, and the pleasure of making an impression. Blessed with a beautiful singing voice—like her niece, the Scottish pop star Lulu—she was rarely far from a song, and her singing filled the house even through her final days.</p>
      <p>To those who knew her, Margaret seemed a woman to whom the world had always opened its doors. Few could have guessed where she began, or how far she had travelled to become the woman they knew. She had not inherited her place in that world. She had made one for herself.</p>
      <p class="sep">· · ·</p>
      <p class="dropcap">Margaret’s story began in 1929 in Glasgow’s Garngad, an Irish Catholic enclave of soot-blackened tenements so notorious for its poverty that the city would eventually demolish much of it and give the district an entirely new name.</p>
      <p>She was one of seven children. Her mother, Helen Darling Kennedy, died at 31 of an infection that would be readily treated today. Margaret was only five. Her father, Hugh Cairns, drank heavily and was imprisoned more than ten times; a prominent scar across his face hinted at ties to Glasgow’s infamous razor gangs. At home, he could be brutal. Margaret remembered beatings that left bruises she was ordered to conceal.</p>
      <p>The children were left largely to raise one another, scraping together whatever they could to eat. Margaret often skipped school to wander Glasgow’s grand department stores, choosing her own china and sitting on the four-poster beds when no one was looking. Sometimes she would stop outside a beautiful house, imagine that the people inside were her parents, and wave goodbye to a mother who wasn’t there.</p>
      <p>There were pockets of tenderness. After her mother died, Margaret repeatedly ran away to her maternal grandmother’s house, where tea came in gold-edged bone china and biscuits were served on a “great blue plate.” She delighted in sorting through the broken biscuits, setting aside the whole ones for her grandmother and aunt.</p>
      <p>These visits were, strictly speaking, forbidden. Her grandmother, Helen Orr Kennedy, had risen to become the first Worthy Grand Mistress of Scotland’s Orange Order, a Protestant organization opposed to marriage with Catholics. Her daughter Helen’s insistence on marrying Hugh, of Irish Catholic descent, had caused a family scandal. Margaret was not inclined to respect the divide.</p>
      <p>Then came the war, and German bombs over Glasgow. It says something about the bleakness of her upbringing that even the Blitz brought an unlikely reprieve: evacuation to the Scottish countryside, where Margaret and her sister Eleanor discovered a world of beauty far removed from the squalor of the Garngad.</p>
      <p>On the farm in Aberdeenshire where they were evacuated, a visiting American named Mrs. Bolton took a liking to Margaret. She taught her the etiquette of high tea and took her on an excursion to Aberdeen, where she gave her a pound note to spend as she pleased. Before leaving her to explore the shops, Mrs. Bolton fastened her own wristwatch around Margaret’s wrist so she wouldn’t have to ask strangers for the time. Margaret was beside herself with pride. She returned with presents for everyone but herself. Margaret would remember that afternoon for the rest of her life, and especially how beautifully Mrs. Bolton smelled. She resolved to smell just as lovely herself, and by all accounts succeeded.</p>
      <p>Margaret was also a voracious reader, devouring whatever books and magazines she could find, often late into the night. Two teachers took a particular interest in her, one convinced that she had a future as a writer. It was an ambition she would carry, in one form or another, throughout her life.</p>
      <p>After the war, Margaret entered a convent, intending to become a nun. For a poor Catholic girl in Glasgow, it offered what little else could: an education, a roof over her head, a respectable way out. But a life of quiet obedience and plain walls was never going to hold a girl who had already glimpsed beauty and wanted more of it.</p>
      <p>At nineteen, she did take vows—just not the ones intended. She married Henry Bamber, a Canadian of Irish and Scottish parentage whom she had known only briefly amid the postwar celebrations, and crossed the Atlantic with him to begin a new life in Montreal. It put an ocean between her and the brothers and sisters with whom she had survived her childhood. She would return only a handful of times.</p>
      <p class="sep">· · ·</p>
      <p>Through her twenties, Margaret bore and raised three children within that marriage, in a new country, isolated from anyone who had known her before she was Mrs. Bamber. Her husband had suffered a brain injury and was, by every account, a difficult man. She had grown up in the shadow of a troubled father, and now she was living with another—and this time there was no grandmother’s house to run to. In the late 1950s, raised Catholic, with three small children and no family within three thousand miles, she divorced him.</p>
      <p>The years that followed were the hardest of an already hard life, yet Margaret made sure nobody knew it. Not yet thirty, she juggled multiple jobs—including night shifts cleaning downtown offices—and it was still barely enough. Determined that her children would never know the poverty she had, she rented in the affluent Westmount neighbourhood, for the sake of its schools, at a rent she could scarcely afford. And she carried herself there as though she had always belonged.</p>
      <p>Yet her private writings tell another story. In 1958, she described a vast emptiness and ambitions unfulfilled, her mind crowded with words she had never spoken or written. The confidence she presented to the world did not always bring her peace.</p>
      <p>With the 1960s, her circumstances began to change. “I have a rendezvous with the future,” she wrote in 1961. At the decade’s start she married Ted Liontos, a handsome Greek photographer, and their daughter Anthea, her youngest, was born. She began modelling—with that striking red hair and expressive features, it was easy to see why—and briefly co-owned a small boutique. The marriage lasted only a few years, and before long she was on her own again, now with four children.</p>
      <p>Through the 1960s and 1970s, she worked in Montreal’s finest stores, among them Birks, the storied jeweller. The girl who had once wandered Glasgow’s grand department stores now stood on the other side of the counter, as much at home among their treasures as she had once dreamed of being. Suitors were never in short supply, but any man who wanted Margaret without her children was soon shown the door—they came together, or not at all.</p>
      <p>By then, the girl from the Garngad had become a woman of Westmount: glamorous, quick-witted, and perfectly at ease among even its best-connected families. The Glasgow of her childhood was gone from her voice entirely. She spoke properly, deliberately and eloquently, with only a slight Scottish lilt left. And, remarkably for a single mother of the time, Margaret eventually saved enough to buy a house of her own.</p>
      <p>She raised her daughters “never to be impressed by a man who pulled up in a Rolls-Royce.” The way to accomplish that, she reasoned, was to make such things familiar. Posture and manners were drilled into them, but more important was the conviction that no one was inherently their superior. Anthea was presented as a debutante at the St. Andrew’s Ball, a mere generation after the tenements of Glasgow.</p>
      <p class="sep">· · ·</p>
      <p>Margaret had always been a searching soul. She filled notebooks with poetry and reflections on love, loneliness, faith and the difficulty of being happy—writing she addressed to her family, and which she hoped might one day be typed up and submitted. In the 1970s, she discovered Christian Science, and in the teachings of Mary Baker Eddy found a philosophy to which she would devote much of the rest of her life.</p>
      <p>She was no casual believer. She studied with a pen in hand, travelled as far as Oxford for theological instruction, and filled pages with her own interpretations of Eddy’s teachings. To Margaret, the spiritual was not something separate from ordinary life, but the truth beneath its appearances. The beauty she had loved since childhood—the changing seasons, a flowering tree, the smallest kindness—was evidence of something divine. And she was never shy about sharing her convictions. A conversation with a friend, a neighbour or a shopkeeper she had known for ten minutes could turn without warning to the healing power of right thinking.</p>
      <p>Around 1979, Margaret met Georges Kendall, who would become the great, difficult love of her later life. She was a romantic to her bones, capable of fierce devotion and equally fierce disappointment. Her poems about Georges move between tenderness, longing and anger; she could see his faults with remarkable clarity and love him all the same. In one she called him her “dear sweet cruel Georges.”</p>
      <p>Then, in the early 1980s, Margaret suffered the greatest losses of her life. Her son Colin and her daughter Helen died barely a year apart, both still young. Her notebooks preserve the anguish of a mother who waited night after night for word from her troubled daughter, hoping still to bring her home. She had spent decades trying to protect her children from the hardships she herself had known, yet could do nothing to spare them this, or herself the terrible fact of surviving them.</p>
      <p>Georges died in 1984, another loss in a decade already crowded with them. Margaret spent much of the years that followed on the move—back home to Scotland, to Bermuda, for a brief stint in Spring Lake—as if distance might do what time could not. Wherever she went, she wrote: poetry, reflections, and the demanding work of reconciling her convictions with the suffering she had witnessed. Her faith remained profound, though her notebooks make clear that belief did not always come easily, nor did it silence her sorrow.</p>
      <p>She never lost her appetite for beauty, laughter or the company of others. Yet the woman who seemed so entirely at home in the world had spent much of her life searching for peace within herself.</p>
      <p class="sep">· · ·</p>
      <p>By the mid-1990s, when Anthea was raising a young son alone while working in Manhattan, Margaret would not hear of leaving him to strangers. She knew what it meant to raise children without family nearby; her daughter would not have to. Decades after crossing an ocean to make a life for herself, she left for New York to help raise her only grandchild, Sean. She brought with her the convictions she held most dear: good manners, an eye for beauty, her unassailable faith, and the quiet assurance that one need never feel out of place in any company.</p>
      <p>Age did little to temper her. Well into her eighties she still loved a practical joke, never more than at Christmas, when a little Tiffany box in a stocking might turn out to hold a set of false teeth or a few toenail clippings. She never lost her gift for making acquaintances. Left on her own for a few hours in Positano, she came back the best of friends with a local contessa she had met that afternoon. And when something had truly upset Anthea, Margaret would listen patiently, let her get all of it out, and then deliver a single, unsentimental verdict: “So what?”</p>
      <p>In later life, her son Paul gave her a small plot of land in Scotland and, with it, a certificate naming her Lady Margaret. She was touched, though never entirely comfortable with a title she considered rather fraudulent. Nobody else thought so.</p>
      <p>In her final years, age gradually took from Margaret the self-sufficiency she had fought so hard to preserve. For a woman accustomed to being the one others depended upon, accepting their care was perhaps the hardest adjustment of all. Her family remained close, grateful for the chance to return some of what she had given so freely.</p>
      <p>She was predeceased by her beloved children Colin and Helen, and by her six siblings, Eleanor (Nellie), Elizabeth (Betty), James, David (Davey), Hugh Jr. (Hughie) and Colin. She is survived by her son, Paul; her daughter, Anthea; and her grandson, Sean, who will carry her memory forward with love, laughter and gratitude.</p>
      <p>In accordance with her wishes, Margaret will be cremated, and at a private family ceremony her ashes will be scattered where she will join Colin and Helen once more.</p>
      <p>Her wit held to the very end. When nurses explained that they needed her consent to take some CT scans, the former model had one question: “Am I being paid for these pictures?”</p>
